Bundled French WCAG 2.2 titles (W3C authorized translation)
================================================================================

The `titleFr` field on every principle, guideline and success criterion in
src/data/wcag.json (vendored at scripts/vendor/wcag-2.2-fr.json) is the AUTHORIZED
French translation of WCAG 2.2 published by the W3C.

Source: https://www.w3.org/Translations/WCAG22-fr/

Like the English source, WCAG 2.2 (and its authorized translations) is © W3C and is
reused here under the W3C Document License
(https://www.w3.org/copyright/document-license/). ultra11y reproduces only the
principle/guideline/success-criterion TITLES, the success criteria's own NORMATIVE WORDING
and the glossary of defined terms, verbatim, resolved at render time by `--lang fr`. As with
the English source these are a derivative work (HTML converted to plain text, nothing
paraphrased, summarised or reordered), distributed in software under the same W3C Document
License clause quoted above, with the same required notice. Attribution: « WCAG 2.2 — W3C
(traduction française autorisée) ».

================================================================================
Bundled RGAA standards pack (France)
================================================================================

The files src/data/standards/rgaa.json and src/data/standards/rgaa.glossary.json
are the RGAA standards pack, derived from the official Référentiel Général
d'Amélioration de l'Accessibilité (RGAA), version 4.1.2, published by the
Direction interministérielle du numérique (DINUM / DISIC) of the French government.

Source: https://github.com/DISIC/accessibilite.numerique.gouv.fr
        (RGAA/criteres.json, RGAA/glossaire.json)

The RGAA content is published under the Licence Ouverte / Open License 2.0
(Etalab): https://www.etalab.gouv.fr/licence-ouverte-open-licence/

ultra11y restructures this content into a standards pack (composing dotted
criterion ids, deriving plain-text titles, mapping each criterion to bare WCAG
success-criterion ids, stripping glossary HTML) but does not alter its meaning.
Attribution: « RGAA 4.1.2 — DINUM ».

================================================================================
Bundled RGAA implementation guidance
================================================================================

The file src/data/guidance/rgaa.json is an implementation-guidance dataset: short
derived summaries and minimal before/after code examples, each keyed to an RGAA
criterion and the WCAG success criteria it maps onto. Its entry ids mirror the rule
files of the SocialGouv/skills "rgaa-html-css" skill, and the patterns are adapted
from that skill and the official RGAA méthode (DINUM).

Source: https://github.com/SocialGouv/skills (skills/rgaa-html-css/rules)
        https://accessibilite.numerique.gouv.fr/methode/criteres-et-tests/

The underlying RGAA criteria are © DINUM under the Licence Ouverte / Etalab 2.0
(above). ultra11y stores only short derived summaries + minimal illustrative
examples, not verbatim prose. When ingesting another rule source, record its own
license and attribution in the dataset header and in this NOTICE before
redistributing any of its text. Attribution: « RGAA 4.1.2 — DINUM ; patterns adaptés
de SocialGouv/skills ».

================================================================================
Bundled WCAG implementation guidance
================================================================================

The file src/data/guidance/wcag.json is an implementation-guidance dataset keyed
directly to WCAG success criteria, so a country standards pack that ships no
guidance of its own inherits examples through its WCAG mapping. It covers all 55
Level A/AA criteria, including the ten the RGAA dataset never reached — six of
them added in WCAG 2.2, after RGAA 4.1.2 (wcagVersion 2.1) froze.

Nothing in it is written by hand. It is GENERATED by
scripts/build-guidance-wcag.mjs from the W3C's own documents, and
`node scripts/build-guidance-wcag.mjs --check` fails if the committed file does
not reproduce byte-for-byte from the vendored snapshot:

  - which techniques implement a criterion: understanding/understanding.11tydata.js
    (the W3C's own machine-readable association map)
  - the summary: the "In brief" block (Goal / What to do / Why it's important) of
    understanding/<version>/<slug>.html, verbatim
  - the code samples: the first HTML or CSS example in the technique and in the
    documented failure, verbatim

Source: https://github.com/w3c/wcag
Spec:   https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/

WCAG 2.2 is © W3C, reused under the W3C Document License, whose derivative-works
clause for software is quoted above; the same required notice applies. The code
examples are "Code Components" as defined by that license and are covered by the
W3C Software License. Attribution: « WCAG 2.2 — W3C ».

================================================================================
Inspiration
================================================================================

The audit report format is inspired by the RGAA audit conventions of DINUM and
the etalab-ia/skills RGAA skill; the native-HTML-first authoring rules are
adapted from the SocialGouv/skills rgaa-html-css skill. No source code was
copied from either project; only public conventions and rule lists were adapted.

================================================================================
ACT Rules Format test methodology (W3C)
================================================================================

ultra11y's ACT consistency suite (tests/fixtures/act/, tests/act-suite.test.ts)
is structured after the W3C ACT Rules Format (passed / failed / inapplicable
expectations, per-rule consistency). The test HTML there is ORIGINAL, authored
for ultra11y. The ACT Rules Format is © W3C. Reference:
        https://www.w3.org/TR/act-rules-format/

